const researchBatchTopics = [
  ['hr-case-intake-privacy-controls', 'HR Case Intake: Privacy Controls for Consistent Triage', 'A practical evidence-first model for routing HR questions while limiting unnecessary employee-data exposure.', 'NIST describes the Privacy Framework as a way to manage privacy risk; the operational implication is to classify the request before collecting more data.', 'Use a structured intake with category, urgency, approved requester, minimum necessary facts, owner, due date, and escalation reason.', 'Which requests need escalation? Anything involving suspected discrimination, safety, accommodation, legal interpretation, payroll correction, or a security incident.'],
  ['hr-document-retention-review-cycle', 'HR Document Retention: Designing a Review Cycle', 'How small HR teams can turn retention rules into a repeatable review and disposition workflow.', 'The Department of Labor and EEOC both publish recordkeeping requirements, showing why retention should be mapped to record type and jurisdiction rather than one blanket timer.', 'Create a register with record class, authority, retention trigger, system of record, owner, legal hold flag, and approved disposition evidence.', 'What is the safest default? Pause disposition when a legal hold, investigation, audit, or unresolved employee matter may apply, then escalate to the responsible owner.'],
  ['hr-employee-change-request-audit-trail', 'Employee Change Requests: Building an Audit Trail', 'A control map for job, manager, location, status, and compensation-change administration.', 'GAO’s Green Book frames documentation as evidence that supports internal control; for HR administration, each approved change should leave a traceable request and reviewer signal.', 'Separate request, validation, approval, system update, reconciliation, and employee notification. Record timestamps without copying sensitive case details into general logs.', 'Can an administrator approve a change? Only when the written delegation says so; otherwise route it to the named HR or management owner.'],
  ['hr-absence-tracking-data-quality', 'Absence Tracking Data Quality: Definitions Before Dashboards', 'Why dependable absence reporting starts with shared definitions and an exception queue.', 'BLS methodology materials demonstrate that a measure is only useful when its population, period, and definitions are explicit.', 'Define absence type, qualifying event, unit, start and end rules, source, correction authority, and aggregation method before publishing a report.', 'How should disputed entries be handled? Preserve the source value, open an exception, record the owner, and avoid silently overwriting history.'],
  ['hr-policy-acknowledgment-evidence', 'HR Policy Acknowledgments: Evidence That Is Easy to Reconcile', 'A workflow for assigning, tracking, and reconciling policy acknowledgments without over-collecting data.', 'NIST’s Privacy Framework emphasizes governance of data processing; a status record usually proves completion without retaining unnecessary copies of the policy recipient’s personal information.', 'Version each policy, assign the correct audience, record assignment and completion signals, route exceptions, and archive the final reconciliation.', 'What counts as completion? Use the approved system’s completion event and policy version, not an informal reply or screenshot in a chat channel.'],
  ['hr-employee-file-quality-check', 'Employee File Quality Checks: A Risk-Based Sampling Method', 'A lightweight sampling method for finding missing, stale, or misplaced HR records.', 'NIST SP 800-53 treats assessment as an ongoing control activity; sampling lets a small team test quality without granting broad access to every record.', 'Choose a documented sample, test required fields and access boundaries, log defects by category, assign remediation owners, and retain aggregate results.', 'Should every file be copied for review? No. Inspect in the approved system and record findings at the minimum level needed for remediation.'],
  ['hr-offer-approval-workflow-controls', 'Offer Approval Workflow Controls for Recruiting Teams', 'Where approval, version control, and candidate-data boundaries matter in offer administration.', 'The EEOC recordkeeping guidance supports preserving employment records, while FTC guidance highlights the need to protect personal information; offer workflows need both record and privacy controls.', 'Lock the approved requisition, route compensation and terms for authorized review, preserve the final version, and keep candidate communications in the approved system.', 'Who owns legal or policy questions? The designated HR or legal owner; the coordinator should not improvise a term or make a selection decision.'],
  ['hr-employee-survey-governance', 'Employee Survey Governance: Useful Aggregates, Safer Handling', 'A practical design for collecting workplace feedback while protecting confidentiality and interpretability.', 'The NIST Privacy Framework centers risk management, so survey governance should define purpose, access, aggregation, retention, and follow-up before launch.', 'Document purpose, audience, minimum demographic breakdown, suppression rule, responsible analyst, response window, and action owner.', 'Can every comment be shared with managers? No. Apply the approved confidentiality and access rules, and escalate threats or sensitive disclosures.'],
  ['hr-knowledge-base-content-controls', 'HR Knowledge Base Controls: Keeping Answers Current', 'How to govern HR help content so answers remain owned, dated, and safe to reuse.', 'ISO management-system guidance and NIST CSF governance principles both support named ownership and review evidence for controlled operational information.', 'Give each article an owner, scope, jurisdiction, effective date, review date, source authority, escalation boundary, and change history.', 'What should a coordinator do with an outdated answer? Mark it for review, link the approved source, and escalate instead of silently editing policy language.'],
  ['hr-timekeeping-exception-management', 'Timekeeping Exceptions: A Reviewable Queue for Payroll Support', 'A structured approach to missing punches, schedule mismatches, and approval exceptions.', 'DOL recordkeeping guidance requires employers to retain specified hours and wage records; exception handling should make corrections traceable to an authorized reviewer.', 'Classify the exception, preserve the original signal, obtain the correction rationale, route approval, update the system of record, and reconcile the payroll feed.', 'May an administrator infer hours? No. Request evidence and escalate unresolved or disputed entries to the responsible manager or payroll owner.'],
  ['hr-background-check-coordination-controls', 'Background-Check Coordination: Separating Process From Decision', 'A boundary-focused workflow for coordinating background checks without making employment decisions.', 'The FTC states that employers using consumer reports have obligations under the FCRA; process coordinators should therefore track consent and notices while leaving decisions to the authorized owner.', 'Use an approved vendor, verify authorization, restrict report access, track required notices, and document the decision owner without reproducing report contents.', 'What must be escalated? Disputes, adverse-action questions, jurisdictional differences, and any request to bypass required notices.'],
  ['hr-remote-worker-records-access', 'Remote Worker Records Access: A Least-Privilege Review', 'A role-based approach to remote access for HR records and workflow tools.', 'CISA identifies identity and access management as a core practice; the measurable HR control is a current role-to-task mapping with review evidence.', 'Separate read, edit, export, and administrative permissions; use named accounts; review role changes at onboarding, transfer, and offboarding.', 'Should shared credentials be allowed? No. Escalate any workflow that depends on shared or untraceable access.'],
  ['hr-exit-interview-data-handling', 'Exit Interview Data Handling: From Notes to Actionable Signals', 'How to preserve useful exit themes while minimizing exposure of confidential employee comments.', 'NARA records-management guidance reinforces the need for defined records and lifecycle decisions; exit data should have a purpose, owner, access boundary, and retention decision.', 'Separate operational completion from confidential notes, code themes consistently, report aggregates, restrict raw comments, and document follow-up ownership.', 'When should raw comments be shared? Only under the approved purpose and access rules; safety, legal, or security concerns should be escalated immediately.']
] as const;

